  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on qualitative analysis of three European case studies, which may limit their direct applicability to highly different governance and planning systems elsewhere.",
      "what": "This study introduces the Relational Placemaking for Inclusion (RPI) framework, which analyzes inclusive urban development across three dimensions: temporality, institutional resonance, and design agency. The framework is applied to three European case studies to examine how civic relationships, institutional adaptation, and participatory mediation shape public space co-production.",
      "why": "It shifts the focus of placemaking evaluation from temporary participatory events and physical outputs to long-term relational capabilities. This provides a structured way to understand how civic initiatives can permanently transform municipal institutions and sustain inclusive practices beyond individual project lifecycles."
    }
